# The ledger of `archive`, its lists of 2026-11-16: what Go's `archive/tar`
# and Go's `archive/zip` (Go 1.27, `go doc` of 2026-11-16) and Python's
# `tarfile` and `zipfile` (3.15) let a program do, one block per capability,
# and what Rebel answers. Rust's standard library has no archives, so its line
# is `-` in every block. The lists are this machine's, 2026-11-16: `go doc
# -all` on both packages, and `__all__` and `dir()` of both modules and of
# their classes. The tests read archives
# Python made; the cross-check in the scratch folder had Python read every
# archive Rebel wrote (tar, tar.gz, zip, 65,536 members, a 4.4 GB member)
# and Rebel extract Python's.

capability archive/tar-read  a tar archive read entry by entry, plain or gzip
  heroes  archive.tar_reader, archive.next_tar_entry, archive.read_tar_data, archive.read_tar_rest, archive.Next, archive.Compression, archive.decode_tar, archive.TarMember
  go      tar.NewReader, tar.Reader, tar.Reader.Next, tar.Reader.Read, tar.ErrHeader, tar.ErrInsecurePath
  rust    -
  python  tarfile.open, TarFile, TarFile.next, TarFile.extractfile, TarFile.fileobject, TarFile.gzopen, TarFile.taropen, TarFile.OPEN_METH, tarfile.ReadError, tarfile.HeaderError, tarfile.StreamError, tarfile.TarError, tarfile.CompressionError, TarFile.ignore_zeros, TarFile.errorlevel
  note    the end is a case of `Next`, never a failure. A block of zeros ends the archive, and a header after it is refused; Python's `ignore_zeros` (concatenated archives) is not offered. A reader takes a `limit` on the archive's bytes after gzip, which neither Go nor Python has. `decode_tar` reads an archive held in memory whole, every entry with its data, where Go reads one over a `bytes.Reader` and Python over an `io.BytesIO`; no file is written on the way

capability archive/tar-header  what a tar header says of an entry
  heroes  archive.TarEntry, archive.tar_entry, archive.Kind
  go      tar.Header, Header.Typeflag, Header.Name, Header.Linkname, Header.Size, Header.Mode, Header.Uid, Header.Gid, Header.Uname, Header.Gname, Header.ModTime, Header.AccessTime, Header.ChangeTime, Header.Devmajor, Header.Devminor, Header.Xattrs, Header.PAXRecords, Header.Format, tar.TypeReg, tar.TypeRegA, tar.TypeLink, tar.TypeSymlink, tar.TypeChar, tar.TypeBlock, tar.TypeDir, tar.TypeFifo, tar.TypeCont, tar.TypeXHeader, tar.TypeXGlobalHeader, tar.TypeGNULongName, tar.TypeGNULongLink, tar.TypeGNUSparse
  rust    -
  python  TarInfo, TarInfo.name, TarInfo.path, TarInfo.size, TarInfo.mtime, TarInfo.mode, TarInfo.type, TarInfo.linkname, TarInfo.linkpath, TarInfo.uid, TarInfo.gid, TarInfo.uname, TarInfo.gname, TarInfo.devmajor, TarInfo.devminor, TarInfo.pax_headers, TarInfo.isfile, TarInfo.isreg, TarInfo.isdir, TarInfo.issym, TarInfo.islnk, TarInfo.ischr, TarInfo.isblk, TarInfo.isfifo, TarInfo.isdev, TarInfo.replace, tarfile.REGTYPE and the other type constants
  note    PAX records apply to their fields (`path`, `linkpath`, `size`, `uid`, `gid`, `uname`, `gname`, `mtime` with its fraction); the others, `atime`, `ctime`, `comment`, `SCHILY.xattr.*`, stay in `records` and are written back. A type this reader does not know is read as a file, as POSIX and Python read it

capability archive/tar-formats  the layouts of a tar header, read and written
  heroes  archive.next_tar_entry, archive.write_tar_header
  go      tar.Format, tar.FormatUnknown, tar.FormatV7, tar.FormatUSTAR, tar.FormatPAX, tar.FormatGNU, tar.Format.String
  rust    -
  python  tarfile.USTAR_FORMAT, tarfile.GNU_FORMAT, tarfile.PAX_FORMAT, tarfile.DEFAULT_FORMAT, TarFile.format, TarInfo.create_ustar_header, TarInfo.create_gnu_header, TarInfo.create_pax_header, TarInfo.create_pax_global_header
  note    v7, ustar, old GNU and PAX are read, with GNU's long names and base-256 numbers; ustar is written, with a PAX entry before a header whose name, link, size, ids, owner names or time ustar cannot hold, as Go's writer chooses. GNU's layout is not written: PAX says all it says

capability archive/tar-sparse  sparse members, whose holes are not stored
  heroes  refused: their data is not laid out as their size says, and writing one back means making holes in a file; a sparse member fails `unsupported`
  go      tar.TypeGNUSparse, Header.Format sparse maps (read)
  rust    -
  python  TarInfo.sparse, TarInfo.issparse
  note    -

capability archive/tar-write  a tar archive written entry by entry, plain or gzip
  heroes  archive.tar_writer, archive.write_tar_header, archive.write_tar_data, archive.write_tar_entry, archive.finish_tar, archive.encode_tar
  go      tar.NewWriter, tar.Writer, tar.Writer.WriteHeader, tar.Writer.Write, tar.Writer.Flush, tar.Writer.Close, tar.ErrWriteTooLong, tar.ErrFieldTooLong, tar.ErrWriteAfterClose
  rust    -
  python  TarFile.addfile, TarFile.close, tarfile.open(mode="w"), tarfile.open(mode="w:gz"), TarInfo.tobuf, TarInfo.frombuf, TarInfo.fromtarfile
  note    data past an entry's size, or a header written while data is owed, fails `invalid`, as Go's ErrWriteTooLong and its padding check do. The end is two blocks of zeros, as Go writes it; Python pads to 10,240 bytes, which readers do not need

capability archive/tar-from-disk  entries made from files on the disk
  heroes  archive.add_tar_file, archive.add_tar_tree
  go      tar.FileInfoHeader, tar.Header.FileInfo, tar.FileInfoNames, tar.Writer.AddFS
  rust    -
  python  TarFile.add, TarFile.gettarinfo, TarFile.dereference
  note    a link is added as a link, never followed (Python's `dereference=False`); a file that grows or shrinks while it is read fails `invalid` rather than writing an archive whose sizes lie. Owner names are not looked up: the system's user database is `os`'s

capability archive/zip-read  a zip archive's directory read, and its members' data
  heroes  archive.zip_reader, archive.ZipReader, archive.entry_of, archive.read_zip_entry, archive.decode_zip
  go      zip.NewReader, zip.Reader, zip.Reader.File, zip.Reader.Comment, zip.Reader.Open, zip.OpenReader, zip.ReadCloser, zip.ReadCloser.Close, zip.File, zip.File.Open, zip.File.DataOffset, zip.ErrFormat, zip.ErrChecksum, zip.ErrAlgorithm, zip.ErrInsecurePath
  rust    -
  python  zipfile.ZipFile, ZipFile.infolist, ZipFile.namelist, ZipFile.getinfo, ZipFile.read, ZipFile.open, ZipFile.comment, ZipFile.testzip, ZipFile.fp, zipfile.BadZipFile, zipfile.BadZipfile, zipfile.error, zipfile.is_zipfile
  note    the stream must seek, as both need a seekable file, or the archive is bytes held in memory (`decode_zip`), as Go's `zip.NewReader` over a `bytes.Reader` and Python's `ZipFile` over an `io.BytesIO` read it, with no file on the way; data prepended to the archive (a self-extractor) is allowed, as both allow it. A member is decompressed to no more than its stated size and its CRC-32 and size are checked at its end, as Go's checksum reader does; `limit` bounds what `read_zip_entry` answers. `testzip` is reading every member

capability archive/zip-header  what the central directory says of a member
  heroes  archive.ZipEntry, archive.zip_entry, archive.Method
  go      zip.FileHeader, FileHeader.Name, FileHeader.Comment, FileHeader.NonUTF8, FileHeader.CreatorVersion, FileHeader.ReaderVersion, FileHeader.Flags, FileHeader.Method, FileHeader.Modified, FileHeader.ModifiedTime, FileHeader.ModifiedDate, FileHeader.CRC32, FileHeader.CompressedSize, FileHeader.UncompressedSize, FileHeader.CompressedSize64, FileHeader.UncompressedSize64, FileHeader.Extra, FileHeader.ExternalAttrs, FileHeader.Mode, FileHeader.SetMode, FileHeader.ModTime, FileHeader.SetModTime, FileHeader.FileInfo, zip.FileInfoHeader, zip.Store, zip.Deflate
  rust    -
  python  ZipInfo, ZipInfo.filename, ZipInfo.orig_filename, ZipInfo.date_time, ZipInfo.compress_type, ZipInfo.compress_level, ZipInfo.comment, ZipInfo.extra, ZipInfo.create_system, ZipInfo.create_version, ZipInfo.extract_version, ZipInfo.reserved, ZipInfo.flag_bits, ZipInfo.volume, ZipInfo.internal_attr, ZipInfo.external_attr, ZipInfo.header_offset, ZipInfo.CRC, ZipInfo.compress_size, ZipInfo.file_size, ZipInfo.is_dir, ZipInfo.from_file, ZipInfo.FileHeader, zipfile.ZIP_STORED, zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED
  note    a name without the UTF-8 flag is read as code page 437 unless it is UTF-8 already, as many writers leave the flag off; Go keeps its bytes. The time is the extended timestamp where there is one, else the DOS time read as UTC (it holds no zone). The mode is Unix's where the archive was made on Unix. Versions, flags, disk numbers and internal attributes are the format's plumbing and are not shown

capability archive/zip-methods  methods other than stored and deflated, and encryption
  heroes  pending: methods 12, 14 and 93 (bzip2, LZMA, Zstandard), whose formats Rebel holds (`compress/bzip2`, `compress/xz`, `compress/zstd`, at layer 4 below `archive`'s 5), and encrypted members are not read or written yet; such a member fails `unsupported`
  go      zip.RegisterCompressor, zip.RegisterDecompressor, zip.Compressor, zip.Decompressor, zip.Reader.RegisterDecompressor, zip.Writer.RegisterCompressor
  rust    -
  python  zipfile.ZIP_BZIP2, zipfile.ZIP_LZMA, zipfile.ZIP_ZSTANDARD, ZipFile.setpassword, ZipFile.open(pwd=), ZipFile.read(pwd=), ZipFile.extract(pwd=)
  note    until 2026-11-21 this block said the formats were not in Rebel, which stopped being true when `compress/bzip2`, `compress/xz` and `compress/zstd` landed (the coherence review's L-D16). Whether zip's LZMA framing (a version and a properties header before a raw stream) is what `compress/xz.decompress_lzma` reads is unrun

capability archive/zip-write  a zip archive written member by member, a member whole or its data in pieces
  heroes  archive.zip_writer, archive.write_zip_entry, archive.begin_zip_member, archive.write_zip_data, archive.end_zip_member, archive.finish_zip, archive.encode_zip, archive.ZipMember
  go      zip.NewWriter, zip.Writer, zip.Writer.Create, zip.Writer.CreateHeader, zip.Writer.Close, zip.Writer.Flush, zip.Writer.SetComment
  rust    -
  python  ZipFile(mode="w"), ZipFile.writestr, ZipFile.open(mode="w"), ZipFile.close, ZipFile.comment
  note    names past ASCII are written in UTF-8 and flagged so; every member says it was made on Unix, with its mode, and carries the extended timestamp. A member written whole has ZIP64's fields exactly where a size, an offset or the count passes 32 bits, as Go writes them. A member written in pieces (`begin_zip_member`, Go's `Create` and Python's `open(mode="w")`) has a data descriptor after its data, and ZIP64's sizes from its header on where the size it was begun with is -1 or within 5 % of 32 bits, as Python judges it (`force_zip64` where the size is not known), so a reader of the descriptor learns its width from the header; Go decides at the member's end instead. Past 32 bits in a member begun smaller is refused, as Python refuses it

capability archive/zip-from-disk  members made from files on the disk
  heroes  archive.add_zip_file, archive.add_zip_tree
  go      zip.Writer.AddFS, zip.FileInfoHeader
  rust    -
  python  ZipFile.write, ZipFile.mkdir, ZipInfo.from_file, zipfile.PyZipFile, PyZipFile.writepy
  note    a file's data is read and compressed 65,536 bytes at a time, with a data descriptor after it, so a file larger than memory is added. PyZipFile's compiling of Python sources is Python's own

capability archive/zip-copy  a member copied from one archive into another without decompressing
  heroes  refused: the member's compressed bytes would be written as they are, with no check that they are what their header says; reading and writing it again checks them
  go      zip.Writer.Copy, zip.Writer.CreateRaw, zip.File.OpenRaw, zip.Writer.SetOffset
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    -

capability archive/zip-append  members added to an archive that is already there
  heroes  refused: rewriting a central directory in place risks the archive on a failure; a program writes a new archive from the old one's members
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  ZipFile(mode="a"), ZipFile(mode="x")
  note    -

capability archive/zip-path  a zip archive walked as a tree of paths
  heroes  refused: `entries` holds every member with its name, which `path` cuts; a second view of the same list is a second way
  go      zip.Reader.Open (fs.FS)
  rust    -
  python  zipfile.Path, Path.exists, Path.is_dir, Path.is_file, Path.is_symlink, Path.iterdir, Path.joinpath, Path.match, Path.glob, Path.rglob, Path.name, Path.filename, Path.parent, Path.stem, Path.suffix, Path.suffixes, Path.relative_to, Path.open, Path.read_bytes, Path.read_text
  note    -

capability archive/extract  an archive extracted into a folder, nothing landing outside it
  heroes  archive.extract_tar, archive.extract_zip
  go      zip.ErrInsecurePath, tar.ErrInsecurePath
  rust    -
  python  TarFile.extractall, TarFile.extract, ZipFile.extractall, ZipFile.extract, tarfile.data_filter, tarfile.tar_filter, tarfile.fully_trusted_filter, TarFile.extraction_filter, tarfile.FilterError, tarfile.AbsolutePathError, tarfile.OutsideDestinationError, tarfile.AbsoluteLinkError, tarfile.LinkOutsideDestinationError, tarfile.SpecialFileError, tarfile.LinkFallbackError, tarfile.ExtractError, TarFile.makedir, TarFile.makefile, TarFile.makelink, TarFile.makelink_with_filter, TarFile.makedev, TarFile.makefifo, TarFile.makeunknown, TarFile.chmod, TarFile.chown, TarFile.utime
  note    one rule, Python's `data_filter` and stricter: an absolute name, a drive, a `..` part anywhere (Python allows one that stays inside), a link absolute or climbing out, a hard link to anything not already inside, a path through a link already on the disk, a device or a pipe, all refused with `invalid`; set-id, sticky and others' write bits dropped; owners never set. Go only reports `ErrInsecurePath` and leaves extraction to the program. A zip's names and total size are checked before anything is written; a tar, read as a stream, stops at the first refusal

capability archive/list  the entries of an archive, without their data
  heroes  archive.list_tar, archive.ZipReader
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  TarFile.getmembers, TarFile.getnames, TarFile.getmember, TarFile.list, ZipFile.printdir, ZipFile.namelist
  note    printing a listing is the program's: it has the entries

capability archive/detect  which archive some bytes begin
  heroes  archive.format_of, archive.Format
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tarfile.is_tarfile, zipfile.is_zipfile
  note    zip by its first signature (Python looks for the end record; a self-extractor begins with a program, and `zip_reader` reads it anyway); tar by a header whose checksum holds

capability archive/other-compressions  tar compressed by bzip2, xz or Zstandard
  heroes  pending: the three formats are Rebel's (`compress/bzip2`, `compress/xz`, `compress/zstd`, at layer 4 below `archive`'s 5) and are not read or written through a tar yet; gzip is offered
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  TarFile.bz2open, TarFile.xzopen, TarFile.zstopen, tarfile.open(mode="r:bz2"), tarfile.open(mode="r:xz"), tarfile.open(mode="r:zst")
  note    -

dropped
  go      tar.Header.Format, tar.FormatUnknown: which layout a header was read in; the fields are what a program uses
  go      zip.ReadCloser, zip.OpenReader: a reader that owns its file; here the caller opens and closes it with `fs`
  python  TarFile.debug, TarFile.errors, TarFile.encoding, tarfile.ENCODING, TarFile.tarinfo, TarInfo.chksum, TarInfo.offset, TarInfo.offset_data, TarInfo.tarfile, TarInfo.get_info: plumbing of the reader, an encoding switch (names here are UTF-8, other bytes read as Latin-1), and offsets inside the archive
  python  ZipFile.fp, ZipFile.mkdir(mode=), ZipInfo.reserved, ZipInfo.volume: the open file object and fields of the format that hold nothing a program reads
  python  zipfile.LargeZipFile, ZipFile(allowZip64=): ZIP64 is written where it is needed, always
  python  tarfile.main, zipfile.main, `python -m tarfile`, `python -m zipfile`: commands of the interpreter, not the library
